A||||8||fiero_nightmare||too many mod idea's||||1||
Z||000000||fiero_nightmare||11-06-2001||06:13 AM||||lately i have been having tons of idea's on modding fiero's. the have been everything from trans am hood scoops to round 3rd brake lights. <P>here are the two i like the most.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/exhaust_2.jpg"> <P>this idea is to take out the muffler and put a strait pipe there. then cut off the clamps on a couple catalytic converters and put them on each exhaust pipe. then get some sheet metal and drill the holes so it will go in between the clamps and cut a hole in the center. that way you can simply adjust how much back pressure you have just by taking out 4 bolts and switching metal plates.<P>the second idea is a notchback body mod.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/14_MODS.jpg"> <P>all this would require is using rivets to hold sheet metal on the rear clip and then doing a lot of fiberglass work over the metal.    <P>what do you think of them? are they bad idea's or not?<P>------------------<BR>my 85 GT pics <P>http://communities.msn.com/russcars/85fierogt.msnw||||reg||1
Z||000001||Cozmo||11-06-2001||06:41 AM||||Original!<BR>As far as the exhaust thing, measuring and calibrating it would be a real bear.  You'd need to dyno it to get it tweaked properly.  <BR>I don't know about the body mod...<BR>When Pontiac did the fastback GT they severely changed the shape of the rear end to balance the lines. By adding extended sail panels it makes the tail look too short and boxy. Might have to address that to make it look OK. Also, visibility would really suck with solid sail panels.<BR>On my next project car I'm going to put a fastback GT rear end on a notchback roof.<BR>(lotsa cutting and fiberglass!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">)<P>   <P>------------------<BR><B>BobC</B><BR><B>'85 2M6 Targa</B><BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/targsig.jpg"> <BR><B>Suncoast Fieros</B>||||reg||14
Z||000002||gtsonly||11-06-2001||12:58 PM||||I like the idea and have given much thought myself to eliminating the muffler and still maintain duel exhaust.  Where the pipe comes off the cat on the passenger side and splits to create the duel exhaust,  path of least resistance will allow the gases to stay on the passenger side mostly.  There will be a flow of exhaust on the drivers side but they would never be equal. I have given the thought of a porsche style exhaust where the split to the duals is in the center, but then you run a clearance problem under the engine. Minds wondering now!!!!!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/myfiero001_copy_1.jpg"> ||||reg||5
